<td id="S4.T2.2.30.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">80.2</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.31" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.2.31.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini-2.5-Pro</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.31.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.31.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">96.6</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.31.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.31.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">96.2</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.31.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.31.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">94</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.31.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.31.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">92.0</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.31.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.31.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">98.9</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.31.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T2.2.31.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">84.0</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Performence across different scales of large language models on the TMBench. Models under 8B parameters struggle with even the simplest reasoning steps, consistent with emergent abilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>. * indicates widely recognized reasoning benchmarks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present a series of experiments conducted on TMBench using a diverse set of models and analytical approaches. The experiments and their results are outlined as follows. First, we introduce the dataset and the experimental setup related to LLMs. Next, we evaluate a broad range of recent open-source LLMs, spanning from 1B to 72B parameters, alongside proprietary models such as Gemini, Grok, and Claude. We then assess the correlation between the TMBench Pass Rate and the Reasoning Score, which is averaged across AIME2024, MATH500, and GPQA. Finally, we conduct several ablation studies, including unbounded-step ablation, temperature ablation, alphabet ablation, and difficulty ablation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a brief overview of the experimental setup, including the datasets and the latest large language models evaluated. Detailed information is provided in the released code and data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span> Based on the dataset design methodology presented in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Design of Turing Machine Bench ‣ 3 Turing Machine Bench ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we constructed TM-Bench to evaluate the mutli-step instruction following capabilities of LLMs. The difficulty levels are determined by three parameters: the number of unique alphabets, the maximum initial queue size, and the length of the production rules, which are set to 5, 5, and 9, respectively. Experiments under other parameter settings can be found in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs.</span> We conduct our evaluations on a diverse set of state-of-the-art large language models (LLMs) ranging from 1B to 72B parameters, covering multiple model families and architectures to ensure a comprehensive analysis.
Our selection includes the instruct-tuned versions of the LLaMA models, specifically the 1B, 3B, 8B, and 70B variants, released by the LLaMA team <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. From the Qwen family, we evaluate both the instruct and preview versions of the 14B, 32B, and 72B models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, along with the language model component of the QVQ-72B-Preview multimodal system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. For the Gemma family, we include both Gemma3-12B and Gemma3-27B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>.
We also incorporate the QwQ-32B-Preview and Sky-T1-32B-Preview model from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>. In addition, we evaluate several proprietary models, including Gemini-2.5-Pro<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, Grok-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, Claude-3.7-Sonnet-Thinking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, Doubao-1.5-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, and OpenAI-o1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>.
For efficient and high-throughput inference, we leverage the Transformers library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> alongside vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, which provides optimized execution for large-scale model evaluations. Unless otherwise specified, we employ a greedy decoding strategy with a temperature of 0 to ensure deterministic outputs and set the maximum generation length to 16384 tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Computation Reasoning Evaluation</h3>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20771v1/0407_TM_bench_chat.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Illustration of step-wise accuracy on TMbench across a diverse set of both open-source and proprietary LLMs. Step-wise accuracy decreases as the number of simulation steps increases. Proprietary models exhibit stronger computation reasoning abilities.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the computation reasoning capabilities of the latest LLMs by analyzing their performance across three metrics: <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SWA Uniform</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">SWA Linear</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pass Rate</span>. These metrics provide a comprehensive assessment of the models’ robustness throughout the chain of thought.
Detailed results are summarized in <a href="#S4.T2" title="In 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, and the accuracy–step curves are shown in <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.2 Computation Reasoning Evaluation ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
We observe that Gemini-2.5-Pro exhibits robust accuracy in computational reasoning, achieving over 90% accuracy at 30 steps, which suggests an emergent ability to simulate Turing Machine.
It is found that models smaller than 8B struggle with even the first step. This observation further supports the phenomenon of <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">emergence</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.
The <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">QVQ</span> multimodal model exhibits a notable decline, potentially attributable to its multimodal training process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Correlation with Reasoning Benchmarks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the relationship between computational reasoning ability and real-task reasoning performance, we analyzed the correlation between TMBench Pass Rate and Reasoning Score (averaged over AIME2024, MATH500, and GPQA). We found a strong positive correlation, with a Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.733, as shown in <a href="#S3.F1" title="In 3 Turing Machine Bench ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Notably, for models such as Gemini-2.5-Pro, Grok-3-beta, Claude-3.7-Sonnet, and Doubao-1.5-Pro, their reasoning performance closely aligns with their TMBench Pass Rate, suggesting that achieving advanced reasoning capabilities is closely tied to robust instruction-following abilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unbounded-Step Ablation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gemini-2.5-pro demonstrates strong instruction-following capabilities, achieving a 94% pass rate under a 30-step constraint. To further analyze the upper bound of its instruction-following ability, we conducted an unbounded-step evaluation. Using rejection sampling, we selected 10 samples and ran each for up to 1000 steps. Through budget forcing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, Gemini was prompted to continue generating until failure with maximum token limit of 1,048,576 tokens. The earliest failure occurred at step 16, and the latest at step 683, as shown in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Alphabet ablation. ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>a. As an autoregressive model, Gemini inevitably fails with increasing steps due to its statistical nature, underscoring Gemini’s computational limits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Temperature ablation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact of temperature on model performance, we conduct an ablation study with temperature values ranging from 0 to 3.0, as shown in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Alphabet ablation. ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>b. Performance is stable within the 0–1 range, but degrades as temperature increases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Alphabet ablation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the robustness of large language models (LLMs) across diverse alphabet types, we conducted evaluations using four distinct character sets: <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vanilla letters</span> (e.g., <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a, b, c, d, e</span>), <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Numerals</span> (e.g., <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1, 2, 3, 4, 5</span>), <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Greek letters</span> (e.g., <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>), and <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Special characters</span> (e.g., @, #, $, %, &amp;). As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Alphabet ablation. ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>c, while most models exhibit performance fluctuations depending on the character set, <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gemini-2.5-Pro</span> consistently achieves high accuracy across all categories, including the <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Special characters</span>, which typically present greater challenges. This robustness indicates that <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gemini-2.5-Pro</span> relies on underlying reasoning mechanisms rather than superficial statistical correlations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2504.20771v1/ablation.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/254;" width="476" height="254"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Illustration of ablation results. (a) Performance comparison of different LLMs across various alphabet types (Vanilla, Number, Greek, Special). (b) Ablation results of temperature. (c) Ablation results of Alphabet. (d) Ablation results of difficulty.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Difficulty Ablation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For TMBench, Gemini-2.5-pro achieves a 94% pass rate within 30 steps, with successful simulations extending up to 686 steps, demonstrating its strong computational reasoning capabilities. To further assess this ability, we perform a difficulty ablation study over the alphabet <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Sigma=\{E,1,\#,\$,*\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>#</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">$</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\{E,1,\#,\$,*\}</annotation></semantics></math> with varying values of <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In Alphabet ablation. ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments and Results ‣ Turing Machine Evaluation for Large Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>d.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce Turing Machine Bench (TMBench), a novel benchmarking framework that leverages m-Tag System Simulation to evaluate the computational capabilities of large language models (LLMs). TMBench focuses on multi-step instruction following without relying on external knowledge, while maintaining a consistent level of difficulty across steps.
We evaluate state-of-the-art open-source LLMs (ranging from 1B to 72B parameters), as well as several proprietary models including Gemini, Grok, and Claude, on the TMBench. Our results show that the leading model, Gemini-2.5-Pro, achieves a 94% simulation pass rate, demonstrating strong general-purpose computational ability.
We further analyze the correlation between TMBench performance and recognized reasoning benchmarks, observing a Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.703. This suggests that TMBench is an effective proxy for evaluating reasoning ability, while avoiding dependence on domain-specific knowledge, thus offering a fairer evaluation.
TMBench’s knowledge-agnostic nature limits its correlation to real-world, knowledge-dependent tasks, but this simplicity provides a controlled setting for evaluating the core reasoning abilities of LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
